## Motivation

cpu_export_jax_op correctly treats non-tensor Python arguments as static inputs. A repeated model call can nevertheless create accidental O(model_depth) Pallas/TorchTPU specializations when a caller passes a layer-derived value that does not represent a real kernel variant.

QKVOG exposed this pattern: 62 layer-specific collective IDs produced 62 export misses, while the repaired schedule-owned contract produced 2 misses and 60 hits for the same first eligible graph. PR #697 fixes that kernel and caller contract. The compiler bridge should not silently canonicalize arbitrary static values, because a static value may legitimately change generated MLIR or collective semantics.

## Proposed diagnostic

Add opt-in or low-noise instrumentation around exported JAX/Pallas custom ops that:

1. records cache hits/misses per export site and custom-op name;
2. reports the static-argument fingerprint dimensions responsible for a miss without dumping sensitive values;
3. warns when one export site observes high static-signature cardinality during one compilation session (for example, a configurable threshold defaulting to 16);
4. includes input shape/dtype cardinality separately, so legitimate shape variants are distinguishable from Python-static fragmentation;
5. exposes counters in tests so wrappers can assert bounded cardinality.

The warning should be diagnostic only. It must not drop or rewrite a static argument.

## Acceptance example

A loop exporting the same tensor shapes with a layer_index Python integer should make the diagnostic identify the static dimension and report N misses. A wrapper using one bounded schedule ID should report the true variant count and subsequent hits. Existing exports with model-wide or otherwise bounded static values should remain unchanged.
